---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import StateLandscape from '../components/sketches/StateLandscape.astro';
import DoseResponse from '../components/sketches/DoseResponse.astro';
import DecisionTree from '../components/sketches/DecisionTree.astro';
import Isolates from '../components/sketches/Isolates.astro';
import PerturbLayers from '../components/sketches/PerturbLayers.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Research - Yonatan N. Degefu" description="The questions behind Yonatan N. Degefu's research on melanoma cell states, drug combinations, and treatment response, and the approaches used to investigate them.">
  <div class="research-container">
    <p class="eyebrow page-kicker">Questions behind the work</p>
    <h1>Research</h1>

    <section class="research-overview">
      <p class="intro">
        One idea runs through these projects: how a biological system responds to treatment depends
        on how its parts work together. Those parts might be proteins in a regulatory network,
        pathways inside a cell, or drugs in a combination, and a change in one can ripple through
        the others. Understanding those interactions helps explain why a treatment works, predict
        when it will fail, and decide which options to test next. Below are the questions that
        motivated each project, the challenges they posed, and how I approached them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="projects" aria-labelledby="research-questions">
      <h2 id="research-questions">Questions I investigate</h2>

      <article class="project-card" id="melanoma" aria-labelledby="melanoma-question">
        <div class="project-text">
          <p class="project-context">Cancer cell plasticity · Fallahi-Sichani Lab</p>
          <h3 id="melanoma-question">How does a cancer cell’s starting state shape its response to drugs and other signals?</h3>
          <p>
            Cancer cells with the same DNA can exist in different states and switch between them.
            Because of this plasticity, a population of cancer cells already holds a mix of states
            before treatment begins, and that mix shapes how it responds to a drug or any other outside
            signal. Using melanoma as a model, I want to decode how these states arise and what makes
            a cell move from one to another.
          </p>
          <p>
            I study AP-1, a group of proteins that helps control gene activity, by building mathematical
            models of how its components interact. I compare the models with measurements of individual
            melanoma cells and work with experimental collaborators to test predictions about how
            changes in the network shift cell states.
          </p>
          <p class="project-insight">
            Our work showed that how the AP-1 network is configured helps explain both the variety of
            melanoma cell states and how cells shift between them under treatment.
          </p>
          <div class="keywords" aria-label="Skills used in the melanoma project">
            <span class="keyword">Mechanistic modeling</span>
            <span class="keyword">Single-cell data analysis</span>
            <span class="keyword">Model calibration</span>
            <span class="keyword">Network analysis</span>
          </div>
          <div class="project-links">
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2026.117965" class="link">Cell Reports (2026)</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="project-sketch">
          <div class="sketch-frame"><StateLandscape /></div>
          <figcaption>A cell reads signals from its surroundings, a drug among them, through a gene regulatory network. How that network interprets them shapes which state the cell settles into.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="project-card" id="ap1-perturbation" aria-labelledby="ap1-perturbation-question">
        <div class="project-text">
          <p class="project-context">Ongoing work · Fallahi-Sichani Lab</p>
          <h3 id="ap1-perturbation-question">Which AP-1 factors control a cell’s state, and what happens when we change them?</h3>
          <p>
            Building on the AP-1 model, we perturb AP-1 factors in melanoma cells and measure the
            response across molecular layers, from protein to chromatin to gene expression. I’m
            building statistical learning models that connect these layers to predict how cell states
            change.
          </p>
          <div class="keywords" aria-label="Skills used in the ongoing AP-1 perturbation project">
            <span class="keyword">Multi-omic integration</span>
            <span class="keyword">Multiplexed imaging</span>
            <span class="keyword">Single-nucleus multiome</span>
            <span class="keyword">Statistical learning</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="project-sketch">
          <div class="sketch-frame"><PerturbLayers /></div>
          <figcaption>Perturb AP-1 factors, measure the response across molecular layers, and learn to predict how cell states change.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="project-card" id="drug-combinations" aria-labelledby="dna-damage-question">
        <div class="project-text">
          <p class="project-context">DNA damage response · AstraZeneca</p>
          <h3 id="dna-damage-question">How can we choose which cancer drugs to combine?</h3>
          <p>
            Knowing how a drug works on its own is only part of the problem. To understand a combination,
            we also need to understand how the pathways it targets interact.
          </p>
          <p>
            During my AstraZeneca internship, I developed a model of DNA damage response pathways in
            MATLAB SimBiology. I used simulated experiments to explore drug interactions and dosing
            strategies, connecting proposed biological mechanisms with predicted treatment responses.
          </p>
          <p class="project-insight">
            The model provided a mechanistic rationale for the combination’s effect and informed dosing
            strategies for both drug-sensitive and drug-resistant cells.
          </p>
          <div class="keywords" aria-label="Skills used in the DNA damage response project">
            <span class="keyword">Quantitative systems pharmacology</span>
            <span class="keyword">MATLAB SimBiology</span>
            <span class="keyword">Simulation</span>
            <span class="keyword">Drug interaction analysis</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="project-sketch">
          <div class="sketch-frame"><DoseResponse /></div>
          <figcaption>When drugs act in synergy, the combination reaches the same effect at lower doses. Simulations show where and why.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="project-card" id="tuberculosis" aria-labelledby="tb-question">
        <div class="project-text">
          <p class="project-context">Tuberculosis drug combinations · Aldridge Lab</p>
          <h3 id="tb-question">How can we find better drug combinations without testing them all?</h3>
          <p>
            This work was motivated by the need to improve long-standing tuberculosis treatments as
            bacteria developed resistance. The number of possible drug combinations made testing every
            option impractical. We needed creative ways to measure combinations efficiently and make
            sense of many different drug responses at once.
          </p>
          <p>
            We combined efficient drug-interaction measurements with machine learning to interpret
            high-dimensional data. I worked on automated analysis pipelines and models that linked
            laboratory measurements to treatment outcomes in mouse studies. We also asked whether
            measurements of drug pairs could guide the design of larger combinations. We derived the
            rules from the same features the models use to make predictions, so each prediction can be
            traced back to the measurements behind it instead of staying a black box.
          </p>
          <p class="project-insight">
            These studies identified interpretable rules for assembling combinations and helped
            prioritize treatments for further testing in tuberculosis models. The Cell Reports Medicine
            study was highlighted in an accompanying Preview, in which Patterson and Palmer described it
            as a framework to “rationally prioritize combinations for clinical development.”
          </p>
          <div class="keywords" aria-label="Skills used in the tuberculosis projects">
            <span class="keyword">Efficient experimental design</span>
            <span class="keyword">Automated data analysis</span>
            <span class="keyword">Machine learning</span>
            <span class="keyword">Interpretable prediction</span>
          </div>
          <div class="project-links">
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xcrm.2022.100737" class="link">Cell Reports Medicine (2022)</a>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cels.2021.08.004" class="link">Cell Systems (2021)</a>
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xcrm.2022.100745" class="link">Preview by Patterson &amp; Palmer (2022)</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="project-sketch">
          <div class="sketch-frame"><DecisionTree /></div>
          <figcaption>A model’s predictions broken down into rules for building a combination. One rule: include a pair that is synergistic and a pair that is potent, each in the right growth condition.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="project-card" id="abscessus" aria-labelledby="abscessus-question">
        <div class="project-text">
          <p class="project-context"><i>Mycobacterium abscessus</i> drug interactions · Aldridge Lab</p>
          <h3 id="abscessus-question">Why does the same drug combination work differently across bacterial strains?</h3>
          <p>
            Samples of the same bacterial species can respond differently to antibiotics. We asked which
            drug interactions are shared across samples and which depend on the particular isolate being tested.
          </p>
          <p>
            In collaborative work on <i>Mycobacterium abscessus</i>, I helped map drug interactions and
            compare responses across isolates. This work identified promising combinations while showing
            why variation between bacterial samples matters when evaluating a treatment.
          </p>
          <div class="keywords" aria-label="Skills used in the Mycobacterium abscessus project">
            <span class="keyword">Drug interaction analysis</span>
            <span class="keyword">Comparative data analysis</span>
            <span class="keyword">Dose-response analysis</span>
          </div>
          <div class="project-links">
            <a href="https://doi.org/10.1128/aac.00090-23" class="link">Antimicrobial Agents and Chemotherapy (2023)</a>
          </div>
        </div>
        <figure class="project-sketch">
          <div class="sketch-frame"><Isolates /></div>
          <figcaption>The same combination can clear some isolates of a species and miss others.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
